Here's how the `Worker` class in `worker.ts` manages job lifecycle:

## Starting Work (`pump`)

The `pump(id)` method is the entry point for starting a job:

```ts
pump(id: string) {
  if (this.abandoned.has(id)) return false;
  this.active.add(id);
  return true;
}
```

It first checks whether the job ID is in the `abandoned` set. If it is, `pump` returns `false` and the job **does not start**. Otherwise, it adds the ID to the `active` set and returns `true`. This is the mechanism that **prevents an abandoned job from starting again** — once a job is marked abandoned, `pump` will always reject it.

## Finishing Work (`finish`)

The `finish(id)` method simply removes the job from the `active` set:

```ts
finish(id: string) {
  this.active.delete(id);
}
```

This marks the job as no longer running. Note that `finish` does **not** add the ID to the `abandoned` set — it just clears it from `active`.

## Preventing Re-start (`step`)

The `step(id)` method is what marks a job as abandoned:

```ts
step(id: string) {
  this.abandoned.add(id);
  this.active.delete(id);
}
```

It adds the ID to the `abandoned` set and removes it from `active`. This is the state change that causes `pump` to reject the job on any future call.

## State Changes vs. Metrics Lookup

| Category | Method | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| **State change** | `pump(id)` | Adds to `active` (if not abandoned); returns `true`/`false` |
| **State change** | `finish(id)` | Removes from `active` |
| **State change** | `step(id)` | Adds to `abandoned`, removes from `active` |
| **Metrics lookup** | `cancelMetrics(id)` | Reads from `counters` map, returns the count (or `0` if absent) |

The key distinction: `pump`, `finish`, and `step` all **mutate** the `active` and/or `abandoned` sets (state changes). `cancelMetrics` is a pure read — it looks up a counter value from the `counters` map and returns it without modifying any state. The `counters` map is separate from the `active`/`abandoned` sets, so metrics tracking is orthogonal to the job lifecycle state machine.

